1
00:00:00,273 --> 00:00:07,011
This is GrantsTrace. It turns GitHub release history into a verifiable grant decision.

2
00:00:07,011 --> 00:00:16,028
The grant administrator sets a repository, a time window, and a threshold. Here, jq needs two stable releases.

3
00:00:16,028 --> 00:00:24,099
Four local nodes independently fetch GitHub releases. Only a candidate prepared for this round can be certified.

4
00:00:24,099 --> 00:00:32,324
At least three nodes must agree. The certified snapshot shows two stable releases, so this grant qualifies.

5
00:00:32,324 --> 00:00:41,019
The designated beneficiary claims one hundred demo credits once. A second claim is rejected. No real money is involved.

6
00:00:41,019 --> 00:00:48,477
Now we inspect another certified deployment. Octocat's Hello World has no releases in the same time window.

7
00:00:48,477 --> 00:00:56,569
Its stable release count is zero, below the threshold of two. The grant is ineligible, and claiming is rejected.

8
00:00:56,569 --> 00:01:06,000
GrantsTrace links independent observation, three of four certification, and one time credit claims under a fixed rule.

